10:25:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No—because labels are not supposed to count arbitrary material that merely touches your mouth. They count ingredients that are **reasonably expected to be consumed and provide usable energy** under normal use. For gum, the unusual part is that “normal use” involves two outcomes at once: - the **base** is chewed and discarded, so it provides essentially no usable calories; - some **sweetener and other soluble ingredients** leave the base, are swallowed with saliva, and can be metabolized. That is not a technicality in the sense of counting invisible, irrelevant matter; it is the same reason a sugar-coated item has calories even if you discard its nonfood support. But the label is necessarily an approximation: it cannot know how long you chewed, how much sweetener dissolved, or how much remained in the wad. So the fair criticism is that a per-piece gum label can make the calorie amount look more precise or personally applicable than it really is. It should be read as a standardized estimate for the product’s consumable ingredients—not as proof that the chewed-out base itself has calories.
