10:26:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That common wisdom wasn’t totally wrong—it was usually shorthand for **“gum is negligible enough that you don’t need to worry about it”**, not a literal claim that every piece contributes exactly zero calories. That idea made sense especially for: - **Sugar-free gum**, which is often only a few calories per piece and may be listed as 0 depending on serving-size rounding rules. - People using gum to curb snacking, where replacing candy or snacks with gum can reduce overall intake. - Normal real-life dieting, where a few calories from an occasional piece are not worth tracking. The more precise version is: - **Spit-out gum base:** essentially zero usable calories. - **Sugar-free gum overall:** usually negligible in normal amounts. - **Sugared gum:** not “free” in the literal sense, because dissolved sugar can be swallowed. - **Two packs a week:** almost certainly too little to matter for weight on its own. So the old advice was practical, not necessarily scientifically exact. It becomes misleading only if “free food” is interpreted as “nothing from gum can ever be absorbed.”
